# What the gameframe-layout port changes on purpose, and why.
#
# Run it as:
#   gate_diff.py --expect tools/porcelain_gate/expect/gameframe-layout.expect <pairs...>
#
# Every line here is a claim a reader is meant to be able to disagree with. If
# you think one of them is wrong, the argument is with the reason, not with the
# tool.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The main modal lands where the layout always said it should.
#
# Classic Fixed states `frame_surface(MODAL, 4, 4, 512, 334)` -- the modal
# shares the scene's box -- on every root, and the provider's own unit test has
# pinned that since it was written. On toplevel 161 it never happened: the old
# apply path converted its canvas rectangle to a parent-local one by asking the
# engine for the modal's PARENT and then that parent's bounds, and when either
# answered anything but OK it returned false and placed nothing at all. The
# modal kept the lane's own centred 130,88 under a frame that had moved
# everything around it.
#
# The port derives the same origin from the element's own two boxes -- the
# drawn one and the parent-local one, whose difference IS the parent's origin
# -- which needs no second lookup and cannot fail that way. So the box moves,
# once, on one lane, to the number the layout has always stated.
#
# This is the one place the port changes what a person sees, and it is the
# defect the ledger's "the modal shares the scene's box" row describes.
only-before BOUNDS com=0x00a10010 (161|16) type=18 graphic=-1 hidden=0 abs=130,88 = the modal at the lane's own centred box, which is where a failed parent lookup left it
only-after  BOUNDS com=0x00a10010 (161|16) type=18 graphic=-1 hidden=0 abs=4,4 = and the box the classic layout has always stated for it
role-move frame_main_modal = the same move, said about the role rather than the component

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The composed art lands in different image slots.
#
# Every box, every size and every hidden bit of every owned control is
# identical. What moved is the internal slot number the client hands a picture,
# because the layer now owns when a picture a DESCRIPTION names is asked for:
# it is requested on the describe that first names it instead of all ninety-
# seven at once from on_start, and everything allocated afterwards renumbers.
#
# THE CLAIM, stated so it can be argued with: a slot id is an internal handle
# and not behaviour. Nothing reads it, nothing persists it, and no two builds
# are obliged to agree on it. This is the same line the minimap-orbs port
# carries, for the same reason, and the same trade is available: byte-identity
# costs keeping every picture resident for the life of the client, which is a
# worse deal than this line.
normalise scene = the image slot is an internal handle, and requesting a picture on the describe that names it renumbers what is allocated after it

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Toplevel 161's DYNAMIC component ids renumber, and nothing else does.
#
# The thirty-one lines below are stated twice, once leaving and once arriving,
# and every pair differs in ONE field: the component id. Type, graphic, hidden
# bit, absolute box, stated size, resize modes, scroll extent and offset are
# identical, the interface is identical, and the count is identical -- which is
# what makes this a renumber rather than a change. The proof is mechanical:
# strip `com=0x... (iface|serial)` from both sides and the two multisets are
# EQUAL, 8 distinct shapes over 31 lines. They are written out
# rather than normalised away because a rule that dropped the component id from
# every BOUNDS line would also stop the gate noticing a component that really
# did appear or vanish.
#
# WHY it renumbers: the id is an allocation serial the client hands out as
# scripts create components, and the port creates twenty fewer transient owned
# controls while the frame comes up -- the old provider built a plan, applied
# it, and rebuilt on the next pass, where the described one names each key once
# and the layer creates it once. Everything the lane's own scripts allocate
# after that point is twenty lower. The 896 block moves further because the
# chatbox's line components are allocated on a different fence for the same
# reason.
#
# THE CLAIM: a dynamic component id is a serial and not an identity. Nothing
# in the cache, the protocol or a save file names one; the boxes are what the
# player sees and they are the same to the pixel.

only-before com=0x0000a79b (0|42907) = a dynamic component id is an allocation serial: the same component, the same box, at a different number
only-before com=0x00a1a40b (161|41995) = a dynamic component id is an allocation serial: the same component, the same box, at a different number
only-before com=0x00a1a40c (161|41996) = a dynamic component id is an allocation serial: the same component, the same box, at a different number
